  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct approach is to use the <b>StorageResolution parameter set to 1</b> with the <b>put-metric-data</b> command when sending your custom metric data via the AWS CLI. This explicitly tells CloudWatch that your metric is high-resolution and should be stored at a one-second interval, instead of the default standard resolution (one minute).<br><br><b>Why the other choices are incorrect:</b><br><i>No changes are required; AWS CLI put-metric-data publishes metrics with a default 1-second resolution.</i> — This is incorrect because the default metric resolution for custom and AWS-built metrics is one minute. You must explicitly set StorageResolution to 1 to achieve a 1-second granularity.<br><i>Add the --dimensions=1 option when sending data with the AWS CLI to enable high-resolution metrics.</i> — This option has no effect on metric resolution. Dimensions are for metadata (like instance ID), not for controlling resolution.<br><i>In the CloudWatch metric graph, change the graph resolution to 1 second and zoom in to observe the metric data at the required detail.</i> — Changing the graph view does not alter how data is published or stored; the resolution is determined at metric creation and publication.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always use the --storage-resolution 1 flag in the AWS CLI for put-metric-data when you want sub-minute (high-resolution) metric data. Without this, your custom metric will only have standard (1-minute) granularity and you won't be able to zoom into the metric at a per-second level, regardless of how you manipulate the CloudWatch interface.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are developing an AWS-based web application. The application requires tracking a custom CloudWatch metric at a finer, one-second granularity, as the usual one-minute interval for AWS-generated metrics is too slow for your requirements.<br><br>What action should you take in order to publish this custom metric data from the AWS CLI at a high (one-second) resolution?</p>",
